1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,080
Can I keep my distance from you for a while?

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:05,360
[DON’T PAINT TILL YOU FAINT. — DEAN]

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:08,640
Miss you.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:09,920
Miss you, too.

5
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:11,200
Your painting is very beautiful.

6
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:12,320
It’s freaking stunning.

7
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:16,600
I can’t be the same person who believed
and painted because of you anymore.

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,280
Because I’ve realized that

9
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:21,280
I should dedicate myself
to what truly matters in my life.

10
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:23,200
I think we should go our separate ways, Mes.

11
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:25,200
Can I stay over at your place tonight?

12
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:26,880
Will you stop being so cute?

13
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,200
If I were so crazy about you
that I lost my mind, what would I do?

14
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:12,880
Making rice porridge?

15
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,400
Mm, yeah.

16
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:17,040
I thought you only knew how to make steak.

17
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:18,800
Come on. That’s an insult.

18
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:20,920
Who eats steak for breakfast?

19
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,200
In the morning, it’s got to be
rice porridge or congee.

20
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:42,079
Well? It’s good, so give me some credit.

21
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:44,800
It’s all right.

22
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:52,680
This rice porridge is made with riceberry
and has dried shrimp and cuttlefish.

23
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:54,440
You don’t find this everywhere.

24
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:58,000
If you want more, you’ll have to
come here for breakfast every morning.

25
00:02:58,480 --> 00:02:59,400
Here only.

26
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:02,480
Nonsense.

27
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,040
Who eats the same thing every morning?

28
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,360
Go sit down.

29
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:17,840
I’ll make coffee
so you can’t accuse me of doing nothing.

30
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:20,200
Okay.

31
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:44,040
Smells good.

32
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:46,680
What? I haven’t even showered yet.

33
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,680
What? I meant the coffee.

34
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,080
Tasty.

35
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:09,360
You mean the coffee?

36
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,400
Of course.

37
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,680
Then what? What else would I mean?

38
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:20,399
Nothing.

39
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:28,480
The coffee you make tastes the best, though.

40
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:55,600
Hey, Duen’s calling.

41
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:56,840
Put her on speaker.

42
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:57,840
Okay.

43
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:00,720
Why are you calling

44
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:03,080
(Dad was hospitalized last night.
He needed emergency surgery.)

45
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:10,680
So…

46
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,080
Is he out of surgery now?

47
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:16,240
(Dad’s gone, Dean.)

48
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:22,400
(There were complications during the surgery.)

49
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:23,960
(The doctors couldn’t save him.)

50
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:27,400
(You should come home, Dean.)

51
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:00,600
Where are the car keys?

52
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:01,680
I can drive…

53
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:10,800
I’ll ask Pun if he’s free to drive.

54
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:14,920
If he’s not, we can get a car service
and go see Duen first.

55
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:19,960
Eat something first.

56
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:26,240
There’s nothing we can do,
even if we go now.

57
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:32,560
You can’t think straight on an empty stomach.

58
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:39,080
Aren’t you in a hurry to get home?

59
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:41,960
Eat first.

60
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:44,480
We’ll figure things out later.

61
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:14,400
Do you want my help?

62
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:15,920
It’s fine.

63
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:18,080
I can manage.

64
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:26,160
Just let me know what you decide.

65
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:29,560
I can go with you.

66
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:35,320
We’ve had our meal.
How about some dessert?

67
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,760
Uh… Let me just take you home.

68
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:03,920
It’s all right.

69
00:08:05,960 --> 00:08:07,160
I can book a ride.

70
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:13,440
You should hurry home.

71
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:17,480
Mhm.

72
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:09,000
[CHANTING AND CREMATION CEREMONY SCHEDULE]
[DATES: 28, 29, 30 NOVEMBER 2026]

73
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,600
[CHANTING AND CREMATION CEREMONY SCHEDULE]
[DATES: 28, 29, 30 DECEMBER 2026]

74
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:16,080
[YOU’VE POSTED THE WRONG MONTH.]

75
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:17,320
[I’VE CORRECTED IT.]

76
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:31,000
[DATES: 28, 29, 30 DECEMBER 2026]

77
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:43,400
I talked to Ten.

78
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:45,720
We’ll definitely attend the cremation ceremony.

79
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:49,280
But we’ll only attend the final night
of the funeral chanting.

80
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:50,720
The final night?

81
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:53,240
But there are five nights of ceremonies.

82
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:54,720
(Ten is busy,)

83
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:57,920
and we’re still not sure if the plans will change.

84
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:06,600
Is something wrong?

85
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:09,800
Nothing.

86
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:15,280
Let’s have dinner together, then.

87
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:17,720
(I’ll see you.)

88
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:22,560
Okay.

89
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Who was that?

90
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:32,600
It’s Moth.

91
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:35,000
He was asking when we’d be
attending Dean’s father’s funeral.

92
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:37,080
I thought Moth had already gone with Dean.

93
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:39,680
That’s how it’s supposed to be,

94
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:43,480
but if things went that smoothly,
they wouldn’t be this couple.

95
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Wow, this looks delicious.

96
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:52,400
Ridiculous.

97
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,080
How can raw chicken look delicious?

98
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:58,360
Not the chicken.

99
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:00,880
I meant you.

100
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:05,280
Shut up.

101
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:06,880
What kind of joke is that?

102
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:09,000
Just kidding.

103
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:11,920
Why are you making so much?
Can we finish all this?

104
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,480
It’s not all for today.
I’m keeping some in the fridge.

105
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:19,280
When you’re busy with work
and don’t have time to eat out,

106
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:23,400
just take some out and pop it
in the air fryer. It’ll be ready in no time.

107
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:27,400
Oh, and there’s also some
mixed vegetable stew in the fridge.

108
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:30,280
Just heat it up whenever you’re hungry.

109
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:36,400
Don’t let me catch you skipping meals
because you’re too busy working again.

110
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:39,800
Yes, sir.

111
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:41,080
Roger that.

112
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:43,400
Whoa.

113
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:47,280
Whose boyfriend is this?

114
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:50,000
So caring. How sweet!

115
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:53,280
But you need to take care of yourself, too.

116
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:55,320
You can’t always rely on me to look after you.

117
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:05,960
Hey.

118
00:12:06,280 --> 00:12:09,480
There. A little reward for cooking for me.

119
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:12,320
Not enough? Want another?

120
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:14,680
Here I go!

121
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:41,800
Ten will attend the funeral today,

122
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:44,040
but he won’t be able to make it again after that.

123
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:47,000
Work’s been hectic for him.

124
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:50,120
His client just moved up the deadline.

125
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:54,000
He asked you to
pass on his condolences to Dean.

126
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:57,880
I don’t even know when I’ll go myself.

127
00:13:01,280 --> 00:13:05,000
Besides, Dean never asked me to come.

128
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:06,480
Are you crazy, Moth?

129
00:13:08,080 --> 00:13:09,800
Who asks someone to come to a funeral?

130
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:14,480
You can go whenever you want.

131
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:16,600
But does he want me there?

132
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:19,200
I mean,

133
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:25,680
would I even be able to help him,
or would I just make him feel worse?

134
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:30,080
Why would you think that?

135
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:36,880
I don’t know.

136
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:41,400
You know what?

137
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,680
I was with him when he found out.

138
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:53,080
And I had no freaking idea what to do.

139
00:13:55,040 --> 00:14:02,000
Dean was going through
such a tough time, and all I did...

140
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:09,080
All I did was stand there and sit there like an idiot.

141
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:13,280
I didn’t know the right thing to say.

142
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:19,000
I wanted to comfort him,
but I didn’t know how.

143
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,800
Damn. The more I talk about it,
the more pathetic I feel.

144
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:27,200
Hey, don’t say that.

145
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:30,400
Don’t think of it that way.

146
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:32,680
I don’t think Dean minded.

147
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:34,840
But I was there.

148
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:41,800
I was right there, so why did I...

149
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,080
Listen to me.

150
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:53,600
Just being there for him was enough.

151
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:57,400
As for everything else,

152
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:02,280
maybe you’re just not
used to dealing with these things yet.

153
00:15:03,200 --> 00:15:07,480
Try a little harder, and you’ll get better at it.

154
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:27,000
[ KITIPHON KITPHONKUN, AGE 66
8 JUL 1960 - 28 DEC 2026 ]

155
00:15:31,720 --> 00:15:34,000
Things are going to get better.

156
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:01,480
What’s up?

157
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:04,160
How are you holding up?

158
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:08,400
I’m…

159
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:10,600
a little busy.

160
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:14,000
(Duen’s taken care of everything.)

161
00:16:14,320 --> 00:16:18,480
From finding a caterer to hiring a florist.

162
00:16:20,280 --> 00:16:21,400
From start to finish.

163
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:25,880
Do you want me there with you?

164
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:36,200
Mm.

165
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:38,280
Come.

166
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:44,200
I’ll be there tomorrow.

167
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:48,880
How are you getting here?

168
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:52,400
I’ll figure it out. Don’t worry.

169
00:16:53,200 --> 00:16:57,400
Take a van to the provincial bus terminal,

170
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:02,800
then book a ride to Duen’s café.

171
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:04,800
Okay.

172
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:08,599
So…

173
00:17:10,599 --> 00:17:12,200
Are you taking the earliest van?

174
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:16,280
Mm-hmm, the earliest one.

175
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:23,680
Okay.

176
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,080
Duen’s still keeping her café open?

177
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,880
I thought she’d be busy
with the funeral arrangements.

178
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:16,000
Yeah. She has plenty of employees.

179
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:18,680
They can help out.

180
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:21,800
Closing the café would mean losing income.

181
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:24,120
Life has to go on.

182
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:33,200
What are you doing?

183
00:19:34,600 --> 00:19:35,680
Dressing up the cat?

184
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:37,080
Look.

185
00:19:37,880 --> 00:19:38,800
How cute!

186
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:49,440
This one’s cute, too.

187
00:19:50,560 --> 00:19:51,000
Oh.

188
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:56,200
It kind of looks like you.

189
00:19:57,080 --> 00:19:57,800
Which part?

190
00:19:58,320 --> 00:19:59,240
Every part.

191
00:20:03,080 --> 00:20:03,880
Fine, it does.

192
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:41,040
[CHANTING AND CREMATION CEREMONY SCHEDULE]

193
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:42,600
Hi, Moth. You’re here.

194
00:20:43,880 --> 00:20:44,720
Good evening.

195
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:47,680
My deepest condolences.

196
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:49,640
Thank you.

197
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:51,800
Let me take you to pay your respects.

198
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:53,680
It’s okay, Mom.

199
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:55,880
I can take him. Don’t worry.

200
00:20:59,080 --> 00:20:59,680
Come on.

201
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:09,160
Here, Moth. Take some fish maw soup.

202
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:10,240
We have plenty left.

203
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:12,440
Whoa, but I’ve already eaten.

204
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:16,200
Go on, have some. Want some bread, too?

205
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:18,920
I’m not going back just yet.

206
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:22,680
I’m planning to stay one more day.

207
00:22:23,040 --> 00:22:23,680
Really, dear?

208
00:22:24,080 --> 00:22:26,960
You can stay at my house.
I’ll make you something to eat.

209
00:22:28,520 --> 00:22:30,000
I remember you like spicy curry.

210
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:31,840
I’ll be right back.

211
00:22:32,680 --> 00:22:33,040
Sure.

212
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:38,120
That’s just how she is.

213
00:22:38,960 --> 00:22:40,160
Just humor her.

214
00:22:40,480 --> 00:22:41,520
She likes taking care of people.

215
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:43,720
She worries about everyone.

216
00:22:45,120 --> 00:22:48,240
That’s why she stayed with Dad for so long.
He liked being taken care of, too.

217
00:22:50,680 --> 00:22:52,320
I figured as much.

218
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:56,960
She must have a lot more free time now,
with no one to look after.

219
00:22:58,880 --> 00:23:00,880
She still has Dean.

220
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:04,600
Oh, him.

221
00:23:05,720 --> 00:23:07,240
Mom doesn’t want to move to Bangkok.

222
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:12,160
Besides, Dean has you taking care of him.

223
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:16,880
No.

224
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:22,600
Dean never asks for much.

225
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:27,840
I know, deep down, Dean is a lot like Dad.

226
00:23:29,160 --> 00:23:30,040
He’s tight-lipped.

227
00:23:30,520 --> 00:23:32,880
He acts nonchalant, like he doesn’t care.

228
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:36,320
But deep inside, he’s very sensitive.

229
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:39,440
He likes to act tough,

230
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:44,120
but what he really wants
is someone who pays attention to him.

231
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:50,760
I was so afraid
no one would ever understand him.

232
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:54,160
But seeing you here puts my mind at ease.

233
00:24:02,760 --> 00:24:05,080
Please take care of my little brother.

234
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:14,480
You’re done for.

235
00:24:18,320 --> 00:24:19,880
I’m not going easy. I’m warning you.

236
00:24:20,320 --> 00:24:22,560
Whoa, he’s taking all the money!

237
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:45,120
Are these pictures for your content?

238
00:24:46,520 --> 00:24:46,960
No.

239
00:24:47,880 --> 00:24:48,800
Just keeping them in my collection.

240
00:24:50,760 --> 00:24:51,400
I’m used to taking pictures.

241
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:53,800
Let me take some of you, too.

242
00:24:54,560 --> 00:24:55,360
The view is beautiful.

243
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:56,720
No.

244
00:24:57,800 --> 00:24:58,720
Come on. Just a few.

245
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:02,600
Why so close?

246
00:25:03,880 --> 00:25:04,840
I can zoom out.

247
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:07,960
Smile.

248
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:09,880
Enough.

249
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:11,880
Why taking so many pictures?

250
00:25:13,920 --> 00:25:14,560
Where should we go next?

251
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:16,800
How about the market near the temple?

252
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:23,240
Aren’t you sad at all?

253
00:25:29,200 --> 00:25:29,720
You think…

254
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:34,120
I should be sadder?

255
00:25:39,080 --> 00:25:39,480
No.

256
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:42,720
That’s not what I meant.

257
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:48,960
Everyone copes in their own way.

258
00:26:00,520 --> 00:26:05,720
Then I guess this is
the saddest face I can make.

259
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:12,720
Were you close to your dad?

260
00:26:15,720 --> 00:26:16,200
No.

261
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:20,760
Well, we used to be.

262
00:26:21,960 --> 00:26:27,960
But after I moved out, we grew apart.

263
00:26:44,640 --> 00:26:46,680
Should we go back together?

264
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:49,200
Sounds good.
There’s nothing else to do here.

265
00:26:49,960 --> 00:26:50,280
Okay.

266
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:53,840
Hey!

267
00:26:54,600 --> 00:26:55,120
What are you doing here?

268
00:26:55,640 --> 00:26:56,800
Who are you paying your respects to, huh?

269
00:26:57,240 --> 00:26:57,800
Get out!

270
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:00,240
The exit’s that way. Go!

271
00:27:00,400 --> 00:27:02,240
Can I say goodbye to him one last time?

272
00:27:02,400 --> 00:27:03,360
Say goodbye? Huh?

273
00:27:04,320 --> 00:27:06,440
Did you love each other so much
that you had to say goodbye?

274
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:08,800
Why?

275
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:10,200
How dare you show your face here?

276
00:27:11,440 --> 00:27:13,840
You have no shame, do you?

277
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:15,080
Dean.

278
00:27:15,480 --> 00:27:16,120
Leave now.

279
00:27:16,680 --> 00:27:17,960
Don’t ever show your face here again.

280
00:27:18,640 --> 00:27:19,320
Especially not to me.

281
00:27:20,160 --> 00:27:23,440
Don’t make me hate my dad

282
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:25,960
or despise you two
any more than I already do.

283
00:27:28,360 --> 00:27:28,640
Go.

284
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:29,960
Dean.

285
00:27:31,400 --> 00:27:32,760
Moth, please take Dean away.

286
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:33,880
Of course, ma’am.

287
00:27:35,560 --> 00:27:36,000
Come on.

288
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:33,680
Thanks.

289
00:28:38,440 --> 00:28:39,320
Have some sugar.

290
00:28:41,320 --> 00:28:42,680
It might make you feel better.

291
00:28:54,480 --> 00:28:57,120
Have you ever felt like you couldn’t breathe,

292
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,440
like the whole world was crumbling
beneath your feet?

293
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:07,120
No.

294
00:29:14,160 --> 00:29:14,840
I have.

295
00:29:21,360 --> 00:29:22,960
When I found out Dad was having an affair.

296
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:29,920
It’s funny.

297
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:33,200
I used to think Mom and Dad were in love.

298
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:40,320
I saw them hug and say
they loved each other every day.

299
00:29:43,880 --> 00:29:45,200
But when I was in junior high,

300
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:49,560
I started noticing that Dad was acting weird.

301
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:52,800
So I followed him.

302
00:29:57,520 --> 00:29:59,000
I saw him with another woman.

303
00:30:02,480 --> 00:30:04,200
He had a whole other life
I knew nothing about.

304
00:30:06,360 --> 00:30:07,480
Dad used to be my hero.

305
00:30:11,280 --> 00:30:14,000
After that, all I saw was a liar.

306
00:30:22,600 --> 00:30:26,240
Hugs weren’t expressions of love.

307
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:34,440
They were expressions of guilt.

308
00:30:38,880 --> 00:30:40,120
The love within my family,

309
00:30:41,960 --> 00:30:45,120
I couldn’t even tell if it was real or fake.

310
00:30:52,160 --> 00:30:53,120
That’s why I hated coming home.

311
00:30:54,840 --> 00:30:55,840
Everything felt like a sham.

312
00:30:58,480 --> 00:31:02,400
I felt bad for Mom and Duen.
They always had to take care of Dad.

313
00:31:05,960 --> 00:31:07,560
That’s why I don’t like
getting attached to anyone.

314
00:31:09,600 --> 00:31:13,960
I’m afraid I’ll turn out to be
a liar just like Dad someday.

315
00:31:21,120 --> 00:31:22,280
That has nothing to do with you.

316
00:31:26,880 --> 00:31:30,840
I believe you’d never be like that.

317
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:53,160
Actually, I don’t think
you should waste your time on me.

318
00:31:57,320 --> 00:31:57,840
What?

319
00:32:00,560 --> 00:32:06,040
Because the truth is,
our relationship is a lie, too.

320
00:32:08,200 --> 00:32:09,280
Just like the story about my dad
I just told you.

321
00:32:13,400 --> 00:32:14,360
It’s different.

322
00:32:14,400 --> 00:32:14,800
It’s the same.

323
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:20,280
I hit on you because I wanted to
give Ten a chance to pursue Pun.

324
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:22,000
And you.

325
00:32:23,120 --> 00:32:27,680
You agreed to be with me because you
wanted Pun to move on from you, right?

326
00:32:32,280 --> 00:32:33,840
We’ve just been using each other as pawns.

327
00:32:34,760 --> 00:32:35,080
Right?

328
00:32:38,560 --> 00:32:39,800
Why are you bringing this up now?

329
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:40,960
Because now’s the best time to face it.

330
00:32:44,560 --> 00:32:47,760
Why don’t we just admit the truth
and go our separate ways?

331
00:32:53,040 --> 00:32:58,080
That’s better than…
lying to each other until we die.

332
00:33:05,160 --> 00:33:06,160
Are you breaking up with me?

333
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:09,440
I don’t know.

334
00:33:11,840 --> 00:33:13,000
Can we even call it a breakup...

335
00:33:17,360 --> 00:33:21,600
when we were never
really together in the first place?

336
00:33:43,640 --> 00:33:44,280
Okay.

337
00:34:11,360 --> 00:34:12,199
Should we go back to your place?

338
00:34:14,320 --> 00:34:15,400
To finish it?

339
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:16,719
No need.

340
00:34:17,080 --> 00:34:17,760
It’s already over.

341
00:34:18,239 --> 00:34:19,239
(But we already have plans.)

342
00:34:19,239 --> 00:34:19,960
Don’t be silly.

343
00:34:20,239 --> 00:34:21,040
Is he really just a senior?

344
00:34:21,920 --> 00:34:23,120
You guys talked for an hour.

345
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:25,560
If you’re going to be like this,
then I don’t want to talk.

346
00:34:26,520 --> 00:34:28,480
Didn’t you hear that he invited me too?

347
00:34:29,040 --> 00:34:32,480
If you f*cking come,
I’ll hate you for the rest of my life.

348
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:45,920
Hey, Moth. Where is Dean?

349
00:34:46,639 --> 00:34:48,040
I don’t know. I’m leaving.

350
00:35:42,200 --> 00:35:42,920
Dean.

351
00:35:43,800 --> 00:35:45,360
You shouldn’t have done that.

352
00:35:47,080 --> 00:35:49,720
What am I supposed to do
to make everyone happy?

353
00:35:51,480 --> 00:35:55,200
Should I just pretend
I don’t know anything, like I used to?

354
00:36:00,680 --> 00:36:03,560
I settled things
with your dad a long time ago.

355
00:36:11,160 --> 00:36:13,400
Nobody’s perfect, Dean.

356
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:18,360
Your dad couldn’t turn back time
and undo what he’d done.

357
00:36:21,800 --> 00:36:25,280
But he’d proved that
he’d chosen not to let go of my hand.

358
00:36:34,040 --> 00:36:35,840
And I forgave him.

359
00:37:13,600 --> 00:37:16,720
That woman started a new family
a long time ago.

360
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:20,080
She just wanted to bid Dad farewell today.

361
00:37:25,400 --> 00:37:27,720
Everyone has moved on.

362
00:37:32,400 --> 00:37:35,240
I won’t blame you if you can’t forgive Dad,

363
00:37:37,360 --> 00:37:41,600
but don’t hold on to it so tightly that you
become afraid to let yourself love someone.

364
00:37:45,120 --> 00:37:51,360
Dean, you truly deserve
to be with someone who loves you.

365
00:38:52,320 --> 00:38:52,920
Hello.

366
00:38:53,760 --> 00:38:54,960
What did you and Dean fight about?

367
00:38:57,080 --> 00:38:58,360
Word got to you fast.

368
00:38:59,640 --> 00:39:03,840
(Ohm and Boom were worried about you,
so they texted me.)

369
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:07,440
So, what’s going on?

370
00:39:09,080 --> 00:39:09,880
I’m going crazy, that’s what!

371
00:39:11,120 --> 00:39:12,680
Being with someone like him
is driving me freaking crazy.

372
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:21,960
I think I’ve had enough.

373
00:39:24,840 --> 00:39:26,640
Why do you guys have to
make everything so difficult?

374
00:39:27,480 --> 00:39:28,360
I don’t get it.

375
00:39:29,880 --> 00:39:31,400
(Other people aren’t like this.)

376
00:39:31,920 --> 00:39:35,320
Maybe things would be easier
if he weren’t in my life.

377
00:39:36,040 --> 00:39:39,760
I mean, you should keep things simple.

378
00:39:40,480 --> 00:39:41,440
Just be straightforward.

379
00:39:42,360 --> 00:39:43,480
How am I difficult?

380
00:39:44,120 --> 00:39:44,840
(Everything is difficult with you.)

381
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:47,160
(Both you and Dean.)

382
00:39:47,560 --> 00:39:48,640
(You two are so difficult.)

383
00:39:53,680 --> 00:39:58,000
(Can’t you just accept how you feel
and be honest with each other?)

384
00:39:59,280 --> 00:40:00,680
It might be hard at first,

385
00:40:03,080 --> 00:40:05,840
but trust me, you can do it.

386
00:40:11,240 --> 00:40:16,480
What happened today alone hurt so much
I almost lost my mind.

387
00:40:18,200 --> 00:40:23,040
(But no matter how much it hurts,
you still love him, right?)

388
00:41:07,800 --> 00:41:09,200
[I’M HIS ONLY SON. ]

389
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:11,080
[…FOR BEING HERE TO SEND HIM OFF
ON HIS FINAL JOURNEY TODAY.]

390
00:41:11,080 --> 00:41:12,280
[HE WAS LOVED BY HIS FAMILY.]

391
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:27,080
Honey. Look.

392
00:41:27,080 --> 00:41:28,600
It’s Dean.

393
00:41:28,600 --> 00:41:30,720
He hurried home to celebrate your birthday.

394
00:41:30,800 --> 00:41:32,600
He must have missed you so much.

395
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:38,280
♪ Happy birthday to you ♪

396
00:42:01,080 --> 00:42:10,640
[WE WEREN’T THAT CLOSE, BUT I STILL LOV…]

397
00:42:56,360 --> 00:42:58,400
On behalf of the Kitphonkun family,

398
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:05,080
I, the only son, would like to
thank you from the bottom of my heart

399
00:43:06,560 --> 00:43:10,240
for being here to send my dad off
on his final journey today.

400
00:43:13,240 --> 00:43:23,440
Dad Kittiphon lived his life with love
and made sacrifices for his family.

401
00:43:24,640 --> 00:43:27,160
He was the warmest, strongest shelter for…

402
00:43:28,680 --> 00:43:33,360
For all of us.

403
00:44:02,960 --> 00:44:04,920
Dad taught me the most important thing:

404
00:44:05,720 --> 00:44:10,520
to always give my best in everything I do.

405
00:44:13,040 --> 00:44:14,800
Show your greatest kindness
to those you choose to be kind to.

406
00:44:16,560 --> 00:44:18,400
Give your greatest love
to those you choose to love.

407
00:44:21,360 --> 00:44:22,560
Because life is short,

408
00:44:25,680 --> 00:44:27,240
and we never know
when we’ll have to part ways.

409
00:44:54,360 --> 00:44:57,360
Dad began his career as a government official in…

410
00:44:59,880 --> 00:45:02,360
199… 3.

411
00:45:03,600 --> 00:45:04,400
I’m sorry.

412
00:45:05,600 --> 00:45:07,000
1987.

413
00:45:08,840 --> 00:45:10,840
And he continued working until his retirement.

414
00:45:39,520 --> 00:45:41,920
I know Dad was never much of a talker.

415
00:45:45,760 --> 00:45:47,400
But he taught me love.

416
00:45:48,720 --> 00:45:55,240
He gave Duen, Mom, and mewarmth and stability.

417
00:45:57,800 --> 00:45:59,000
They were priceless.

418
00:46:02,640 --> 00:46:07,600
He might not have said much,
but all three of us could feel them.

419
00:46:11,880 --> 00:46:14,760
Even though he made mistakes

420
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:20,400
and let people down,especially his own family,

421
00:46:25,960 --> 00:46:32,200
he taught me that nobody in this world is perfect.

422
00:46:35,760 --> 00:46:39,160
We just have to choose what we want to live with.

423
00:46:40,080 --> 00:46:44,800
Will we drown in guilt over the past,
or find the courage to right our wrongs?

424
00:47:00,800 --> 00:47:01,560
Honey.

425
00:47:02,840 --> 00:47:04,080
Honey.

426
00:48:20,840 --> 00:48:24,760
(There may be a thousand reasons why people fall in love.)

427
00:48:27,280 --> 00:48:30,360
(Some reasons might even sound crazy.)

428
00:48:32,320 --> 00:48:33,240
It’s good.

429
00:48:33,440 --> 00:48:35,000
(But it’s when we’re going through
our toughest times)

430
00:48:35,760 --> 00:48:39,200
(that we realize just how much the person in front of us means to us,)

431
00:48:42,400 --> 00:48:48,480
(and how much harder life would be without them.)

432
00:49:41,920 --> 00:49:43,440
You haven’t asked Moth to be your boyfriend yet?

433
00:49:43,760 --> 00:49:45,560
Moth and I are way past that point.

434
00:49:45,880 --> 00:49:48,000
Maybe Moth’s waiting for you to ask him.

435
00:49:48,520 --> 00:49:49,840
I’m applying for a scholarship in New York.

436
00:49:50,080 --> 00:49:50,840
Do you want to go?

437
00:49:54,200 --> 00:49:56,920
I’m afraid I’ll regret it
for the rest of my life if I don’t go.

438
00:49:59,880 --> 00:50:00,680
Can you stay with me?

439
00:50:02,240 --> 00:50:04,280
I want to be honest with my heart.

440
00:50:04,560 --> 00:50:06,480
And don’t I have a place in your heart at all?

441
00:50:06,840 --> 00:50:09,800
Are you sure everything will stay the same?
